#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100617 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dcde3b5-5cdf-5266-9392-0835053a7ded" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of Function Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "2359455c-4a44-5794-8b86-738bcdbbd11d" :question/math-academy-id "e-455" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= x^{2} + 1 \\\\\n\\frac{du}{dx} &= 2x \\\\\ndu &= 2xdx \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}du &= xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule\n\n$$\nu=x^2+1\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & -1 & 0 \\\\ u & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^0 x(x^2+1)^3\\,\\textrm{d}x=\\int_2^1 u^3\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{2}^{1}u^{3} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du &= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{2}^{1}u^{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left.\\frac{u^{4}}{4}\\right|_{2}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1^{4} - 2^{4}}{4} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1 - 16}{4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{15}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: Note that in this example, when we changed the limits the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 \\ldots \\textrm{d}x$ became $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^1 \\ldots \\textrm{d}u$. When changing the limits, it is important to respect the order in which the limits are applied. The lower limit of the integral in $u$ must correspond to the lower limit of the integral in $x$. Same for the upper limit."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100618 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d63e99a-9db4-565e-97c5-ffc3090b0074" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Definite Integral of a Rational Function Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "dd931400-d592-5402-b380-dee7812adac5" :question/math-academy-id "e-456" :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral $\\displaystyle\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{x^2}{x^3+2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= x^{3} + 2 \\\\\n\\frac{du}{dx} &= 3x^{2} \\\\\ndu &= 3x^{2}dx \\\\\n\\frac{1}{3}du &= x^{2}dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule\n\n$$\nu=x^3+2\n$$\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x & -1 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^2 \\dfrac{1}{(x^3+2)}\\,x^2\\textrm{d}x=\\int_1^{10} \\dfrac{1}{u}\\,\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du &= \\frac{1}{3}\\int_{1}^{10}\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{3}\\ln (u)\\right|_{1}^{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(\\ln (10) - \\ln (1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\ln (10)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
